<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  coverage.runsettings - WHAT THE PER-SERVICE COVERAGE GATE MEASURES
  ==================================================================================================

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED

  Constraint C-H of the Agent Action Plan requires 80 percent LINE coverage PER IN-SCOPE SERVICE,
  evaluated per service so that one service's coverage cannot mask another's. The artifact that gate
  reads is the coverage.cobertura.xml each test project emits through coverlet.collector.

  Without this file that report covers every assembly the test run loaded, and for these four
  services that is a much larger set than the service itself: each test project references the
  shared libraries and PowerFramework.Contracts, whose generated protobuf stubs alone run to
  thousands of sequence points that a service's own tests were never meant to exercise. The
  difference is not marginal. Measured on this tree, the report's own top-level line-rate against the
  service assembly's line-rate:

      gateway-service         22.77 percent   versus   PowerFramework.Gateway       88.77 percent
      dataservices-service    50.93 percent   versus   PowerFramework.DataServices  91.19 percent
      persistence-service     34.30 percent   versus   PowerFramework.Persistence   88.05 percent
      security-service        13.39 percent   versus   PowerFramework.Security      91.68 percent

  A gate reading the unfiltered top-level rate would therefore fail all four services while every one
  of them clears the threshold, and it would do so for a reason that has nothing to do with the
  quality of any service's tests. Filtering to the four service assemblies makes the report contain
  exactly one package per run - the service under test, because no other service's assembly is
  loaded by its test project - so THE REPORT'S OWN LINE-RATE IS THE PER-SERVICE NUMBER, and the gate
  needs no aggregation step that could be got wrong.

  WHY ONE FILE RATHER THAN FOUR

  All four assembly filters live in a single Include list, and the four services still measure
  independently. That is a property of the runs, not of the filter: a service's test project loads
  its own application assembly and none of the other three, so the three filters that do not apply
  simply match nothing in that run. One file cannot drift out of step with itself, which is the same
  reason package versions live in one Directory.Packages.props.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FILTERED, EACH FOR A STATED REASON

    * The shared libraries and PowerFramework.Contracts are EXCLUDED from the service gate, not
      untested. They carry their own six test projects with 8278 tests between them, and CI runs
      those in the root leg. Holding a service's number hostage to a shared library's coverage is
      exactly the masking C-H forbids, in the other direction.
    * No source file, type or member is excluded. In particular the EF-generated migrations under
      services/persistence-service/PowerFramework.Persistence/Data/Migrations are counted even though
      C-H speaks of business-logic code and a generated migration is not that. Excluding them would
      raise the Persistence number, and the number clears the threshold without it, so the stricter
      measurement is kept: a gate that has never had its subject narrowed to make it pass is worth
      more than a gate that has.
    * SkipAutoProps is NOT set. Leaving it off keeps the reported figure the plain line rate of the
      service assembly, which is what the numbers above and in docs/BUILD.md were measured as.
    * No branch, method or complexity threshold is declared here. C-H names a line-coverage floor and
      nothing else, and the workflow enforces that one number; a second threshold invented here would
      be a requirement this refactor was not given.
    * No threshold at all, in fact. Coverlet can fail a test run below a threshold on its own, but
      then the failure would read as a test failure rather than a coverage failure, and the run would
      stop before the report was published. The gate belongs in .github/workflows/ci.yml where it can
      report the measured number, publish the report as an artifact, and fail with a message naming
      the service.

  USE

  The literal command line cannot be quoted here, because a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML
  comment and would make this settings file malformed - the same constraint the project files in this
  repository record. So the options are named rather than spelled:

      dotnet test, in Release, passing the settings option with this file's path and requesting the
      "XPlat Code Coverage" data collector

  The path is repository-root-relative; CI passes it explicitly from the repository root so that the
  documented per-service command in docs/BUILD.md, which passes no settings option at all, keeps
  working unchanged for a developer measuring locally. docs/BUILD.md spells both command lines in
  full, where Markdown imposes no such restriction.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <!--
            Cobertura, because that is the format the gate parses and the format the AAP names. A
            second format would produce a second artifact nothing reads.
          -->
          <Format>cobertura</Format>

          <!--
            THE FOUR IN-SCOPE SERVICE ASSEMBLIES, AND NOTHING ELSE.
            Coverlet's filter syntax is [assembly]type; the wildcard covers every type in the
            assembly. Assembly names are matched, not project names, and each of these is fixed by
            the AssemblyName in the corresponding .csproj.
          -->
          <Include>[PowerFramework.Gateway]*,[PowerFramework.DataServices]*,[PowerFramework.Persistence]*,[PowerFramework.Security]*</Include>

          <!--
            The test assemblies themselves are never the subject of a coverage measurement. They are
            already excluded by the Include list above, since none of them is named there; this
            entry states the intent explicitly so that widening Include later cannot silently start
            counting test code as covered production code.
          -->
          <Exclude>[*.Tests]*</Exclude>

          <!--
            Deterministic report location and no merge across runs. Each service's run produces its
            own report under its own results directory, which is what keeps the four measurements
            independent.
          -->
          <SingleHit>false</SingleHit>
          <UseSourceLink>false</UseSourceLink>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
